(Introduction to Vectors in Matlab )Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
13.07011 0 0 13.07011 144.08968 586.24141 Tm
(This is the basic introduction to Matlab. Creation of vectors is include\d with a few )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(basic operations. )Tj
0 -2.55714 TD
(Matlab is a software package that makes it easier for you to enter matri\ces and )Tj
0 -1.20001 TD
(vectors, and manipulate them. The interface follows a language that is d\esigned to )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(look a lot like the notation use in linear algebra. In the following tut\orial, we will )Tj
T*
(discuss some of the basics of working with vectors. )Tj
0 -2.55714 TD
(To start matlab, open up a unix shell and type the command to start the \software: )Tj
/T1_3 1 Tf
0 -1.2 TD
(matlab)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
(. This will start up the software, and it will wait for you to enter you\r )Tj
T*
(commands. In the text that follows, any line that starts with two greate\r than signs )Tj
T*
(\(>>\) is used to denote the matlab command line. This is where you ente\r your )Tj
T*
(commands. )Tj
0 -2.55714 TD
(Almost all of Matlabs basic commands revolve around the use of vectors. \A vector is )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(defined by placing a sequence of numbers within square braces: )Tj
/T1_4 1 Tf
0 -2.56197 TD
(>> v = [3 1])Tj
0 -2.39999 TD
(v =)Tj
T*
(     3     1)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
0 -3.75233 TD
(This creates a vector which has the label "v". The first entry in the ve\ctor is a 3 and )Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(the second entry is a 1. Note that matlab printed out a copy of the vect\or after you hit )Tj
T*
(the enter key. If you do not want to print out the result put a semi-col\on at the end of )Tj
T*
(the line: )Tj
/T1_4 1 Tf
0 -2.56197 TD
(>> v = [3 1];)Tj
0 -1.2 TD
(>>)Tj
/T1_0 1 Tf
0 -2.55232 TD
(If you want to view the vector just type its label: )Tj
